#slide[
  === Hold that comparison --- it is the map for everything that follows.

  #set text(20pt)

  - The three regimes disagree about *who decides* that a conflicted deal is clean: a court applying equity, or a drafter following a checklist. #pause

  - Delaware's competitive advantage was always the first answer. Its product was the case law --- and the judges who made it. #pause

  - Nevada and Texas are selling the second answer, and in 2025 Delaware moved toward it. #pause

  - Watch the convergence: § 144(b)--(c) now does for controllers something close to what RMBCA § 8.61 has long done for conflicted directors. #pause

  #callout(
    marker: [#emoji.quest],
  )[If a statute can specify in advance what makes a conflicted transaction clean, what exactly was the Court of Chancery for?]
]

== SB 21

#slide[
  === SB 21 (signed March 25, 2025) rewrote § 144 and narrowed § 220.

  #set text(20pt)

  - *Safe harbors.* Cleansing for interested directors, officers and controllers. #pause

  - *Definitions.* Statutory tests for "controlling stockholder," "control group" and "disinterested director." #pause

  - *Independence presumption.* Listing-standard independence presumes disinterest. #pause

  - *Controller exculpation* (§ 144(d)(5)). No monetary damages against a controlling stockholder as such --- except for loyalty, bad faith or intentional misconduct, and improper personal benefit. A § 102(b)(7) for controllers, with no charter opt-in required. #pause

  - *Books and records.* An enumerated list; more only on compelling need. #pause

  - *Retroactivity.* Reaches prior acts, except matters pending on February 17, 2025.

  #callout(
    marker: [#emoji.quest],
  )[Was SB 21 even needed --- could the legislature simply have waited for the courts?]
]

#slide[
  === The core move: for most controller deals, one protection now suffices.

  #set text(19pt)

  #table(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr, 1fr),
    align: (left, left, left),
    stroke: 0.5pt,
    inset: 10pt,
    table.header([*Transaction*], [*Before SB 21*], [*After SB 21*]),
    [Controller transaction with a non-ratable benefit (non-freeze-out)],
    [_Match_: entire fairness unless all six _MFW_ conditions met; one device shifts burden only],
    [§ 144(b): safe harbor on *either* disinterested-director committee approval *or* a majority-of-the-minority vote],

    [Going-private / freeze-out],
    [_MFW_: both protections, _ab initio_, or entire fairness],
    [§ 144(c): safe harbor requires *both* --- committee approval *and* a majority-of-the-minority vote],
  )

  - The committee must have at least two directors. Delaware kept _MFW_'s *architecture* for freeze-outs --- both devices --- and abandoned _Match_ for everything else. It did not keep _MFW_'s *timing*.

]

#slide[
  === What § 144 did not keep: the _ab initio_ requirement.

  #set text(19pt)

  - Under _MFW_ and _Synutra_, both protections had to be in place *before substantive economic negotiations began*; commit late and the safe harbor was gone. #pause

  - § 144(b)(2) requires only that the deal be conditioned on the disinterested-stockholder vote "as in effect at the time it is submitted to stockholders." Neither § 144(b) nor § 144(c) contains an _ab initio_ requirement. #pause

  - A far later moment: the controller may negotiate price first and add the cleansing condition on the way to the proxy statement. #pause

  #callout(
    marker: [#emoji.quest],
  )[If _ab initio_ was what made the minority's veto real --- pricing the deal rather than blessing it --- has § 144(c) kept _MFW_, or only its vocabulary?]

  #text(
    size: 0.8em,
  )[DGCL § 144(b)(2) (quoted); the timing rule is _MFW_ as construed in _Flood v. Synutra International_ (Del. 2018). See Shapira, _SB 21 and the_ Ab Initio _Requirement_, Harv. L. Sch. F. on Corp. Governance (July 15, 2026).]
]

#slide[
  === Who counts as a controller is now a statutory question with a floor.

  #set text(20pt)

  - A controlling stockholder is one who either:

    - holds a majority of voting power, or the power to elect a majority of directors; *or*

    - holds at least *one-third* of voting power *and* has the *power to exercise* managerial authority over the business and affairs of the corporation. #pause

  - The second prong is the operative change: below one-third, no amount of practical influence makes you a controller. #pause

  #callout(
    marker: [#emoji.quest],
  )[Is a bright line the right instrument here, given that the abuse the doctrine targets is precisely the exercise of influence without formal control?]
]

